          "AWS Organizations establishes a hierarchical tree of Organizational Units (OUs) rooted at a Management Account, enabling centralized billing, automated account vending, and declarative governance guardrails across hundreds of member accounts.",
          "At the pinnacle of multi-account governance sit Service Control Policies (SCPs): JSON policy documents that define the maximum available permissions for an account or entire OU tree.",
          "It is mathematically crucial to understand that an SCP never grants permissions by itself; instead, an SCP acts as an immutable filter or permission ceiling: effective permissions represent the strict intersection `P_effective = P_identity ∩ P_scp ∩ P_boundary ∩ P_resource`.",
          "If an identity-based IAM policy grants `AdministratorAccess` (`*.*`), but an attached OU SCP explicitly denies `s3:DeleteBucket`, any delete bucket attempt is instantaneously rejected because explicit denies always override allows.",
          "Furthermore, IAM Permission Boundaries prevent privilege escalation by junior engineers or automated CI/CD roles: an administrator sets a boundary on an IAM role, ensuring that even if the role creates new sub-roles, those sub-roles can never exceed the boundary ceiling.",
          "Analyze the formal four-way IAM evaluation engine model below computing effective permissions across SCPs, boundaries, and identity policies.",
          "In production enterprise environments, account vending machines (AVMs) provision isolated accounts for each autonomous product team, automatically establishing security guardrails, VPC endpoints, and baseline IAM roles during account creation.",
          "By enforcing strict OU boundaries, security teams ensure that compliance mandates (such as HIPAA, PCI-DSS, or SOC 2) are enforced uniformly without relying on individual engineers to manually configure bucket encryption or security groups."
