          "Security Groups are Stateful: if traffic is allowed inbound on port 443, return traffic is automatically permitted outbound on the ephemeral port, regardless of outbound rules.",
          "In stark contrast, NACLs are Stateless: they evaluate inbound and outbound packets independently! If a subnet NACL allows inbound TCP 443 from `0.0.0.0/0`, but outbound rules do NOT allow return traffic to the client's ephemeral port range (`1024-65535`), the connection will immediately hang and time out!",
          "NACL rules are processed in strict numerical order (from rule 1 to 32766): the first rule that matches the packet's protocol, port, and IP range determines the outcome (ALLOW or DENY), and evaluation halts immediately without evaluating higher-numbered rules.",
          "Implement the deterministic stateless dual-direction NACL evaluation engine below.",
