          "AWS IAM governs cloud infrastructure APIs: writing to S3, querying DynamoDB, or publishing to SQS. The IAM Role Trust Policy specifies the OIDC provider and the exact Kubernetes ServiceAccount name in its `StringEquals` condition: `\"oidc.eks.us-east-1...:sub\": \"system:serviceaccount:production:payment-processor\"`.",
          "If an attacker tampers with the pod definition to use a different namespace (e.g. `development:payment-processor`), AWS STS rejects the role assumption because the namespace claim does not match the trust policy.",
          "Implement the dual-layer Kubernetes RBAC and AWS IAM trust policy validation engine below.",
          "Dual-layer authorization enforces strict boundaries: Kubernetes RBAC governs cluster-internal APIs, while AWS IAM trust policies govern cloud resource access.",
          "Validating namespace and ServiceAccount claims in trust policies guarantees that development workloads cannot impersonate production payment roles."
