          "To guarantee high availability across failure domains, Kubernetes provides Topology Spread Constraints: declarative rules that instruct the scheduler to distribute pods evenly across zones, nodes, or racks.",
          "A Topology Spread Constraint enforces a mathematical invariant governed by `maxSkew`: the maximum allowable difference in pod counts between any two topology domains `maxSkew = max(domain_count) - min(domain_count)`.",
          "By configuring `topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone` with `maxSkew: 1` and `whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule`, an architect guarantees that no single Availability Zone can host more than one pod more than any other zone, preventing single-AZ concentration disasters.",
          "Analyze the mathematical maxSkew distribution invariant model across 3 availability zones below.",
