          "Composite sort keys structured with hierarchical delimiters (`USER#100`, `ORDER#2026-11-12`) facilitate complex range queries and 1-to-many relationship traversals in a single Query call.",
          "Designing access patterns prior to schema definition ensures that all relational queries map directly to primary key lookups or targeted Global Secondary Index queries.",
          "To satisfy multiple disparate access patterns across the same data (e.g. querying 'orders by customer' vs querying 'orders by status across all customers'), Single-Table Design leverages Global Secondary Indexes (GSIs).",
          "A Global Secondary Index defines an alternate Partition Key (`GSI1PK`) and Sort Key (`GSI1SK`) across the table data.",
          "DynamoDB replicates items asynchronously to GSIs in near-real-time (sub-10ms): when an item is written with `GSI1PK = STATUS#PENDING` and `GSI1SK = 2026-11-12T10:00:00`, a fulfillment worker can query `GSI1` to fetch all pending orders across all customers in chronological order!",
          "Furthermore, DynamoDB supports Sparse Indexes: an item only appears in a GSI if the item actually possesses the GSI partition key attribute.",
          "If 99% of orders are completed and only 1% are pending review, adding `GSI1PK = REVIEW_REQUIRED` only to the 1% creates an ultra-compact sparse index, slashing GSI storage and query costs by 99%!",
          "Study the Global Secondary Index projection and sparse index query simulator below."
